# contributor: GitHub operations for a Dagger coding agent.
#
# The principle: an agent should operate GitHub the way a human at a
# terminal does — with the `gh` CLI. One general `gh` tool replaces the
# bespoke tools this module used to grow one at a time (ghApi, submitPr,
# updatePr, openIssue, …): every verb gh has is available at once, the
# agent already knows the CLI's shape from its training, and its output is
# already written for a reader. `gh pr view 123`, `gh issue list --label
# bug`, `gh pr checks`, `gh issue create --body-file tmp/body.md` — no
# schema to learn, no tool to add for the next verb. `--json`/`--jq` are
# for extracting one field, `gh api` for what the CLI has no verb for.
#
# The `gh` tool mounts the whole workspace (worktree and .git) into a
# throwaway container and runs from its root, so `--body-file <path>` can
# name a file the agent drafted with its editor, and git — hence gh —
# sees the checkout's origin remote. The mount is a snapshot: nothing gh
# does writes back into the workspace. A denylist keeps gh from touching
# the account, the gh installation, or repository-level settings.
#
# A few operations stay bespoke, each for a concrete reason:
#
# - `comment` posts to an issue or pull request — on the conversation,
#   anchored to a file/line of a PR's diff, or as a reply inside a review
#   thread. Comments are agent-authored by definition, so the tool enforces
#   a ":robot:" body prefix telling readers a machine wrote it; `gh pr
#   comment`/`gh issue comment`/`gh pr review` are denied so the marker
#   can't be skipped.
#
# - `updateComment` edits an existing comment or description with
#   editor-style exact-match replacement — surgical, never a rewrite.
#   `gh pr edit --body` replaces the whole text (and still trips over
#   GitHub's retired Projects-classic GraphQL fields), so it's denied in
#   favor of this.
#
# - `reviewComments` renders a PR's review threads with their file, line,
#   and diff hunk. `gh pr view --comments` shows conversation comments only;
#   the line-anchored threads people leave in reviews have no gh rendering.
#
# - `pushBranch` publishes the workspace's committed HEAD to a branch on a
#   DIFFERENT repository — typically a fork, for when the agent cannot or
#   should not push branches to the repository it checked out. Committer's
#   push only reaches the checkout's own remote; this one names its
#   destination, completing the fork flow: checkout upstream → edit →
#   commit → pushBranch(repo: "you/fork", branch: "fix-x") →
#   `gh pr create --head you:fix-x`.
#
# - `checkout` re-points the agent at a DIFFERENT repository: it fetches a
#   git repo as a Workspace (GitRef.asWorkspace) and returns a continuation
#   of the current conversation bound to that checkout. The agent keeps its
#   own toolset — tools live on the conversation, and their `Workspace!` args
#   simply auto-fill from the new binding — and the target workspace's agents,
#   if it has any, are composed ON TOP (minus modules the originating
#   workspace already provides). A checked-out workspace refuses export by
#   construction, so the only way its changes leave is commit + push. The
#   old workspace's pending edits stay behind unless named in `keep`, which
#   overlays them onto the fresh checkout as uncommitted edits — the escape
#   hatch for scratch work, or a task begun on the wrong branch.
#
# Configuration lives in dagger.toml settings, not per-call arguments:
# `githubToken` authenticates GitHub operations, reaching the container
# only as a secret env var (GH_TOKEN) — never baked into an image layer,
# never echoed. `remote` names the GitHub repository bare issue/PR numbers
# resolve against; it's a property of the project rather than of any one
# task (a permanent fork would simply configure a different value). When no
# remote is configured, the tools fall back to the checkout's own origin
# remote: the toolbox mounts the workspace's git directory at .git, so git —
# and therefore gh — sees the repository it is operating on.

# Tail-truncate command output to keep tool results context-friendly.
let tailLines = 200
# Patches read top-down, so they get head-truncated instead.
let patchLines = 400

# Last `lines` lines of s, with a truncation marker when shortened.
let tail(s: String!, lines: Int! = tailLines): String! {
  let all = s.split("\n")
  if (all.length <= lines) {
    s
  } else {
    let marker = "… (" + toString(all.length - lines) + " earlier lines truncated) …"
    marker + "\n" + all.takeLast(lines).join("\n")
  }
}

# First `lines` lines of s, with a truncation marker when shortened.
let head(s: String!, lines: Int! = patchLines): String! {
  let all = s.split("\n")
  if (all.length <= lines) {
    s
  } else {
    let marker = "… (" + toString(all.length - lines) + " later lines truncated) …"
    all.takeFirst(lines).join("\n") + "\n" + marker
  }
}

"""
GitHub operations: run the `gh` CLI against the workspace's checkout, post
agent-marked comments on issues and pull requests and edit them
surgically, read pull request review threads with their diff context, push
to a fork, and switch the conversation to a checkout of another repository.
"""
type Contributor {
  # Private so the secret is never exposed as a field/tool argument.
  let token: Secret = null
  # The GitHub repository bare issue/PR numbers resolve against. Private:
  # configured in settings, not steered per-call.
  let remote: String! = ""

  new(
    """
    A GitHub token used to authenticate `gh` and the GitHub API.

    Wire it up in dagger.toml settings rather than passing it around:

        [env.dev.modules.contributor.settings]
        githubToken = "env://GITHUB_TOKEN"

    Without it, everything that talks to GitHub is unavailable. Git
    operations use the host's own git credentials instead.
    """
    githubToken: Secret = null,
    """
    The GitHub repository this project's issues and pull requests live in,
    as "owner/name" (a github.com URL works too). Like the token, this
    belongs in dagger.toml settings — it's a property of the project, not
    of any one task, so it shouldn't vary between contributors or throwaway
    forks (a permanent fork would simply configure a different value):

        [env.dev.modules.contributor.settings]
        remote = "dagger/dagger"

    When unset, tools that need a repository fall back to the origin remote
    of the workspace's own checkout.
    """
    remote: String! = "",
  ) {
    self.token = githubToken
    self.remote = remote
    self
  }

  """
  Compose the contributor tools onto a base LLM.
  """
  agent(base: LLM!): LLM! @agent {
    base
      .withTools(currentNode)
      .withSystemPrompt(systemPrompt)
  }

  """
  Run the GitHub CLI, `gh`, from the root of the workspace's checkout.

  Use it the way a human at a terminal would: `gh pr view 14174`, `gh pr
  view 14174 --comments`, `gh issue list --label bug`, `gh pr checks 14174`,
  `gh issue create --title "..." --body-file tmp/body.md`, `gh pr create
  --title "..." --body-file tmp/pr.md`, `gh release list`, `gh run view
  <id> --log-failed`. The output is already written for a reader. Reach
  for `--json`/`--jq` only to extract a specific field (an id, a URL, a
  head sha), and `gh api <path>` only for what the CLI has no verb for.

  The whole workspace is mounted, so `--body-file <path>` can name a file
  you wrote with your editor (e.g. under tmp/). The mount is a snapshot:
  gh cannot write into the workspace. Bare issue/PR numbers resolve against
  the configured remote, or the checkout's origin remote when none is
  configured; pass `--repo owner/name` to target another repository.

  Not available here: account, config, secret, key, extension, alias and
  codespace management; `repo delete/archive/rename/edit`; `api` with a
  DELETE method; `pr comment`, `issue comment` and `pr review` (use
  `comment`, which marks the comment as agent-authored); and `pr edit`/
  `issue edit` with `--body`/`--body-file` (use `updateComment`, which
  edits text surgically instead of rewriting it).
  """
  gh(
    source: Workspace!,
    """
    Arguments to `gh`, already tokenized, WITHOUT the leading "gh" — e.g.
    ["pr", "view", "14174"] or ["issue", "list", "--label", "bug"].
    """
    args: [String!]!,
  ): String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): any invocation may mutate GitHub or read something
    # live; never replay a previous result from the session cache.
    let tok = token
    if (tok == null) {
      raise noTokenError
    }
    if (args.isEmpty) {
      raise "gh: pass the subcommand and its arguments, e.g. [\"pr\", \"view\", \"123\"]"
    }

    # The command and subcommand are the first two positional tokens;
    # flags — and the value of the few global-ish flags that take one — are
    # skipped so `gh -R owner/name pr view 1` still reads as `pr view`.
    let positional: [String!]! = []
    let skipNext = false
    let hasRepoFlag = false
    let done = false
    args.each { a =>
      if (done) {
        # Everything after "--" is positional to gh, but never the command.
        null
      } else if (skipNext) {
        skipNext = false
      } else if (a == "--") {
        done = true
      } else if (a.hasPrefix("-")) {
        if (a == "-R" or a == "--repo") {
          hasRepoFlag = true
          skipNext = true
        } else if (a.hasPrefix("--repo=") or a.hasPrefix("-R")) {
          hasRepoFlag = true
        } else if (a == "--hostname") {
          skipNext = true
        }
      } else if (positional.length < 2) {
        positional = positional + [a]
      }
    }
    let cmd = positional[0] ?? ""
    let sub = positional[1] ?? ""

    if (["auth", "config", "secret", "ssh-key", "gpg-key", "extension", "extensions", "ext", "alias", "codespace", "cs"].contains(cmd)) {
      raise `gh ${cmd} is not available from this tool: it manages the gh installation or the GitHub account, not the project.`
    }
    if (cmd == "repo" and ["delete", "archive", "rename", "edit"].contains(sub)) {
      raise `gh repo ${sub} is not available from this tool: repository-level changes are for the human operator to make.`
    }
    if (cmd == "api" and hasMethod(args, "DELETE")) {
      raise "gh api with a DELETE method is not available from this tool: deleting things on GitHub is for the human operator to do."
    }
    if ((cmd == "pr" and (sub == "comment" or sub == "review")) or (cmd == "issue" and sub == "comment")) {
      raise `gh ${cmd} ${sub} is not available from this tool: ` + "use the `comment` tool instead, which marks the comment as agent-authored (\":robot:\") so readers can tell a machine wrote it."
    }
    if ((cmd == "pr" or cmd == "issue") and sub == "edit" and hasBodyFlag(args)) {
      raise `gh ${cmd} edit with --body/--body-file is not available from this tool: ` + "it replaces the whole description. Use `updateComment` to change exactly the text that needs changing."
    }

    # A configured remote is what bare numbers mean; inject it for the
    # subcommands that take --repo, unless the caller named a repository
    # themselves. `gh api` has no --repo: GH_REPO resolves its
    # {owner}/{repo} placeholders instead. Without a configured remote, gh
    # resolves the repository from the mounted checkout's origin.
    let slug = remoteSlug
    let full = args
    if (slug != "" and !hasRepoFlag and ["issue", "pr", "release", "label", "run", "workflow"].contains(cmd)) {
      full = withRepoFlag(args, slug)
    }
    let apiRepo = if (cmd == "api") { slug } else { "" }

    # Plain, non-interactive output: no prompts, no pager, no color, no
    # update nag. The arguments reach gh as argv, never spliced into shell
    # source.
    let script = """
      set -eu
      # Secret commands (e.g. `gh auth token`) commonly emit a trailing
      # newline; HTTP headers reject it.
      GH_TOKEN="$(printf '%s' "$GH_TOKEN" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
      export GH_TOKEN
      if [ -n "$API_REPO" ]; then
        GH_REPO="$API_REPO"
        export GH_REPO
      fi
      exec gh "$@"
    """

    let ran = toolbox(source)
      .withEnvVariable("API_REPO", apiRepo)
      .withEnvVariable("GH_PROMPT_DISABLED", "1")
      .withEnvVariable("GH_NO_UPDATE_NOTIFIER", "1")
      .withEnvVariable("GH_PAGER", "cat")
      .withEnvVariable("NO_COLOR", "1")
      .withEnvVariable("TERM", "dumb")
      # any call may mutate or read something live: never serve it from
      # the exec cache
      .withEnvVariable("CONTRIBUTOR_NONCE", Random.string)
      .withSecretVariable("GH_TOKEN", tok)
      .withExec(["sh", "-c", script, "gh"] + full, expect: ReturnType.ANY).{{exitCode, stdout, combinedOutput}}

    if (ran.exitCode != 0) {
      raise `gh ${full.join(" ")} failed (exit ${toString(ran.exitCode)}):` + "\n\n" + tail(ran.combinedOutput)
    }

    # gh output reads top-down, so head-truncate. Some verbs report only on
    # stderr ("✓ Closed pull request …"); don't lose that when stdout is
    # empty.
    if (ran.stdout.trimSpace == "") {
      head(ran.combinedOutput)
    } else {
      head(ran.stdout)
    }
  }

  """
  Post a comment on a GitHub issue or pull request — on the conversation,
  anchored to a file (and optionally a line) of a pull request's diff, or
  as a reply inside an existing review thread.

  Comments posted this way are agent-authored, so `body` MUST start with
  ":robot:" — the marker that tells readers a machine wrote the comment,
  not the human operator. Calls without the prefix are rejected. Keep it
  kind, brief, and to-the-point.
  """
  comment(
    source: Workspace!,
    """
    The issue or pull request to comment on: a number like "13831"
    (resolved against the configured remote, or the checkout's origin
    remote when none is configured) or a URL like
    "https://github.com/dagger/dagger/pull/13831" or
    "https://github.com/dagger/dagger/issues/13800".
    """
    target: String!,
    """
    The comment body (Markdown), starting with ":robot:".
    """
    body: String!,
    """
    File path within the repository to anchor the comment to, as a review
    comment on that file of the pull request's diff. Empty posts on the
    conversation instead. Pull requests only.
    """
    path: String! = "",
    """
    Line number to anchor the comment to, in the file's post-PR version.
    The line must appear in the PR's diff. 0 anchors to the file as a
    whole. Requires `path`.
    """
    line: Int! = 0,
    """
    An existing review comment to reply to, as the "#discussion_r<id>" link
    GitHub hands out (a bare "discussion_r<id>" or the numeric id work
    too). The reply lands in that comment's thread. Mutually exclusive
    with `path`/`line`.
    """
    replyTo: String! = "",
  ): String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): posting a comment is a side effect, not something to
    # replay from the session cache.
    let tok = token
    if (tok == null) {
      raise noTokenError
    }
    if (!body.trimSpace.hasPrefix(":robot:")) {
      raise robotPrefixError
    }
    if (line != 0 and path == "") {
      raise "a line anchor needs a file: pass `path` along with `line`"
    }
    if (replyTo != "" and path != "") {
      raise "`replyTo` and `path`/`line` are mutually exclusive: a reply lands in an existing thread, a path opens a new one"
    }
    let ref = issueRef(target)
    let replyId = if (replyTo == "") { "" } else { commentId(replyTo) }

    # Replies and file-anchored comments go through the review-comments
    # API (the latter needs the PR's current head commit); plain feedback is
    # an issue comment, which is how GitHub models a conversation comment
    # on issues and pull requests alike. The body travels as a file, never
    # spliced into shell source.
    let script = resolveRepoSh + "\n" + """
      if [ -n "$REPLY_TO" ]; then
        gh api --method POST "repos/$OWNER_REPO/pulls/$NUMBER/comments/$REPLY_TO/replies" \
          -F "body=@/tmp/body.md" --jq .html_url
      elif [ -n "$FILE_PATH" ]; then
        if ! head_sha="$(gh api "repos/$OWNER_REPO/pulls/$NUMBER" --jq .head.sha 2>/dev/null)"; then
          echo "$OWNER_REPO#$NUMBER is not a pull request (or does not exist): file/line-anchored comments need a pull request" >&2
          exit 1
        fi
        set -- api --method POST "repos/$OWNER_REPO/pulls/$NUMBER/comments" \
          -F "body=@/tmp/body.md" \
          -f "commit_id=$head_sha" \
          -f "path=$FILE_PATH"
        if [ "$LINE" -gt 0 ]; then
          set -- "$@" -F "line=$LINE" -f side=RIGHT
        else
          set -- "$@" -f subject_type=file
        fi
        gh "$@" --jq .html_url
      else
        gh api --method POST "repos/$OWNER_REPO/issues/$NUMBER/comments" \
          -F "body=@/tmp/body.md" --jq .html_url
      fi
    """

    let ran = toolbox(source)
      .withNewFile("/tmp/body.md", body)
      .withEnvVariable("OWNER_REPO", ref.repo)
      .withEnvVariable("NUMBER", ref.number)
      .withEnvVariable("FILE_PATH", path)
      .withEnvVariable("LINE", toString(line))
      .withEnvVariable("REPLY_TO", replyId)
      # posting must actually happen every time, not be served from the
      # exec cache
      .withEnvVariable("CONTRIBUTOR_NONCE", Random.string)
      .withSecretVariable("GH_TOKEN", tok)
      .withExec(["sh", "-c", script], expect: ReturnType.ANY).{{exitCode, stdout, combinedOutput}}

    if (ran.exitCode != 0) {
      raise `commenting on ${ref.repo}#${ref.number} failed (exit ${toString(ran.exitCode)}):` + "\n\n" + tail(ran.combinedOutput)
    }

    `Posted comment: ${ran.stdout.trimSpace}`
  }

  """
  Edit a comment, or an issue/pull request description, by exact-match
  replacement — the same semantics as editing a file: `oldText` must occur
  exactly once in the current text and is replaced by `newText`. Include
  enough surrounding context to make the match unique; the rest of the
  text is untouched.

  Comments can only be edited when they are agent-authored (their body
  starts with ":robot:") — human comments are off limits. Descriptions
  (the opening text of an issue or pull request) are editable regardless.
  """
  updateComment(
    source: Workspace!,
    """
    What to edit: a comment link with "#issuecomment-<id>" (a conversation
    comment) or "#discussion_r<id>" (a review comment), or an issue/PR
    reference — a number like "13831" (resolved against the configured
    remote, or the checkout's origin remote) or a URL like
    "https://github.com/dagger/dagger/pull/13831" — to edit its
    description.
    """
    target: String!,
    """
    The exact text to replace. Must occur exactly once.
    """
    oldText: String!,
    """
    The replacement text.
    """
    newText: String!,
  ): String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): reads the live text, then edits it — never replay
    # either from the session cache.
    let tok = token
    if (tok == null) {
      raise noTokenError
    }
    if (oldText == "") {
      raise "`oldText` must not be empty: name the text to replace"
    }
    let ref = editRef(target)

    # Read the current body, let jq do the replacement (it handles
    # arbitrary multi-line text; --rawfile keeps old/new out of the program
    # and the shell), then PATCH it back — the body travels as a file, never
    # spliced into shell source. A comment is only touched when it is
    # agent-marked; a description (issues/N or pulls/N) is fair game. For a
    # description, pulls/N is probed first: an issue 404s there.
    let script = resolveRepoSh + "\n" + """
      case "$KIND" in
        issuecomment) api="repos/$OWNER_REPO/issues/comments/$COMMENT_ID"; guard=1 ;;
        review) api="repos/$OWNER_REPO/pulls/comments/$COMMENT_ID"; guard=1 ;;
        *)
          if gh api "repos/$OWNER_REPO/pulls/$NUMBER" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            api="repos/$OWNER_REPO/pulls/$NUMBER"
          else
            api="repos/$OWNER_REPO/issues/$NUMBER"
          fi
          guard=""
          ;;
      esac
      gh api "$api" >/tmp/current.json
      jq -j --arg guard "$guard" --rawfile old /tmp/old.txt --rawfile new /tmp/new.txt -f /tmp/replace.jq /tmp/current.json >/tmp/body.md
      gh api --method PATCH "$api" -F "body=@/tmp/body.md" --jq .html_url
    """

    let ran = toolbox(source)
      .withNewFile("/tmp/old.txt", oldText)
      .withNewFile("/tmp/new.txt", newText)
      .withNewFile("/tmp/replace.jq", replaceJq)
      .withEnvVariable("OWNER_REPO", ref.repo)
      .withEnvVariable("NUMBER", ref.number)
      .withEnvVariable("KIND", ref.kind)
      .withEnvVariable("COMMENT_ID", ref.commentId)
      # the read must be live and the edit must actually happen every
      # time, not be served from the exec cache
      .withEnvVariable("CONTRIBUTOR_NONCE", Random.string)
      .withSecretVariable("GH_TOKEN", tok)
      .withExec(["sh", "-c", script], expect: ReturnType.ANY).{{exitCode, stdout, combinedOutput}}

    if (ran.exitCode != 0) {
      # jq's error prefix ("jq: error (at /tmp/current.json:0): ") is noise
      # around the reasons replaceJq raises on purpose.
      let why = ran.combinedOutput.replaceMatches(`(?m)^jq: error \([^)]*\): `, with: "")
      raise `updating ${ref.what} failed (exit ${toString(ran.exitCode)}):` + "\n\n" + tail(why)
    }

    `Updated ${ran.stdout.trimSpace}`
  }

  """
  Read the review comments on a GitHub pull request, grouped into threads
  with their file, line, and diff context.

  Accepts a bare PR number (resolved against the configured remote
  repository, or the checkout's own origin remote when none is configured)
  or a full PR URL. A link to a single comment — the `#discussion_r<id>`
  URLs GitHub hands out — narrows the output to just that comment's thread.
  """
  reviewComments(
    source: Workspace!,
    """
    The pull request to read: a number like "13831", a URL like
    "https://github.com/dagger/dagger/pull/13831", or a single-comment link
    like "https://github.com/dagger/dagger/pull/13831#discussion_r3732814956".
    """
    pr: String!,
  ): String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): review threads are live conversations — new replies must
    # show up on the next read, not be replayed from the session cache.
    let tok = token
    if (tok == null) {
      raise noTokenError
    }

    let repo = ""
    let number = ""
    let discussion = ""
    let ref = pr.trimPrefix("#")
    if (ref.containsMatch(`^\d+$`)) {
      repo = remoteSlug
      number = ref
    } else {
      let m = ref.match(`github\.com/([^/]+)/([^/]+)/pull/(\d+)(?:/files)?(?:#(?:discussion_)?r(\d+))?`)
      if (m == null) {
        raise `not a pull request reference: "${pr}" — pass a PR number or a github.com PR URL`
      }
      # Indexing is statically nullable; groups always exist on a match, and
      # the unmatched optional fragment group surfaces as "".
      repo = (m.captures[0] ?? "") + "/" + (m.captures[1] ?? "")
      number = m.captures[2] ?? ""
      discussion = m.captures[3] ?? ""
    }

    # Fetch every review comment on the PR, then let jq group them into
    # threads (replies carry in_reply_to_id pointing at the thread root) and
    # render them — narrowed to one thread when DISCUSSION_ID is set.
    let script = resolveRepoSh + "\n" + """
      if [ -n "$DISCUSSION_ID" ]; then
        printf '## Review thread discussion_r%s on %s#%s\n\n' "$DISCUSSION_ID" "$OWNER_REPO" "$PR_NUMBER"
      else
        printf '## Review comments on %s#%s\n\n' "$OWNER_REPO" "$PR_NUMBER"
      fi
      comments="$(gh api --paginate "repos/$OWNER_REPO/pulls/$PR_NUMBER/comments?per_page=100")"
      printf '%s' "$comments" | jq -rs -f /tmp/render.jq
    """

    let ran = toolbox(source)
      .withNewFile("/tmp/render.jq", renderJq)
      .withEnvVariable("OWNER_REPO", repo)
      .withEnvVariable("PR_NUMBER", number)
      .withEnvVariable("DISCUSSION_ID", discussion)
      # live read: never serve replies from the exec cache
      .withEnvVariable("CONTRIBUTOR_NONCE", Random.string)
      .withSecretVariable("GH_TOKEN", tok)
      .withExec(["sh", "-c", script], expect: ReturnType.ANY).{{exitCode, stdout, combinedOutput}}

    if (ran.exitCode != 0) {
      raise `reading review comments for ${repo}#${number} failed (exit ${toString(ran.exitCode)}):` + "\n\n" + tail(ran.combinedOutput)
    }

    # Threads read top-down, so head-truncate like a patch.
    head(ran.stdout)
  }

  """
  Publish the workspace's committed HEAD to a branch on ANOTHER repository —
  typically a fork, for when you cannot or should not push branches to the
  repository you checked out. The committer `push` tool only reaches the
  checkout's own remote; this one names its destination, so the fork flow is
  checkout upstream → edit → commit → pushBranch → `gh pr create --head
  owner:branch`.

  Pending changes are NOT pushed: commit first. The push API handles
  authentication using the host's own git credentials and respects
  pushInsteadOf URL rewrites (e.g. HTTPS to SSH).

  A push the remote rejects (its branch moved past ours) is an error, never
  a takeover. To overwrite anyway, pass `expectedRemoteSHA`: the push then
  succeeds only while the remote branch still points at that sha — a lease,
  like `--force-with-lease` — so use it only after reading what the remote
  moved to, and only when discarding that is the intent.
  """
  pushBranch(
    source: Workspace!,
    """
    The destination GitHub repository, as "owner/name" (a github.com URL
    works too) — typically your fork of the repository you checked out.
    """
    repo: String!,
    """
    Destination branch name on that repository, e.g. "fix-span-leak".
    """
    branch: String!,
    """
    Lease on the destination branch: omit for a plain push (fast-forwards and
    branch creation only), or pass the sha the remote branch is expected to
    point at to force-update it from exactly that state.
    """
    expectedRemoteSHA: String = null,
  ): String! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): pushing is an effect on the remote; a later identical
    # call must push again, not replay the first result.
    if (branch.trimSpace == "") {
      raise "pushBranch: the destination branch must not be empty"
    }
    let slug = normalizeSlug(repo)
    let url = if (slug.containsMatch(`^[^/:]+/[^/]+$`)) {
      "https://github.com/" + slug
    } else {
      repo
    }
    # The first read of the workspace's git state: a workspace with no
    # readable history gets the plain answer, not a push error.
    source.git.head.commitSHA rescue {
      e: Error => return `This workspace has no readable git history (${plainError(e.message)}); ` + "there is nothing to push. Pending edits are still visible with status / diff."
    }
    let result = source.git.head.push(
      to: git(url),
      branch: branch,
      expectedRemoteSHA: expectedRemoteSHA,
    ).{{ref, previousSHA, sha, disposition}} rescue {
      e: Error => raise `push to ${slug} failed: ${plainError(e.message)}`
    }
    let summary = case (result.disposition) {
      GitPushDisposition.UP_TO_DATE => `${result.ref} on ${slug} is already up to date at ${short(result.sha)}; nothing to push.`

      GitPushDisposition.CREATED => `Pushed ${result.ref} (new branch) to ${slug} at ${short(result.sha)}.`

      GitPushDisposition.FORCED => `Pushed ${result.ref} to ${slug}: forced ${short(result.previousSHA)} -> ${short(result.sha)}.`

      else => `Pushed ${result.ref} to ${slug}: ${short(result.previousSHA)} -> ${short(result.sha)}.`
    }
    let left = source.git.uncommitted.diffStats.{{path}}.length
    let leftNote = if (left > 0) {
      "\n" + `${toString(left)} file(s) remain uncommitted and were not pushed; status shows them.`
    } else {
      ""
    }
    summary + leftNote
  }

  """
  Switch this conversation to a checkout of a DIFFERENT git repository.

  Your workspace is replaced by a fresh checkout of `repo` — every workspace
  tool you already hold (read, edit, status, diff, commit, push, ...) simply
  operates on the new checkout from here on. When the target workspace
  defines agents of its own, they are composed on top of your toolset,
  except modules your originating workspace already provides.

  The switch is one-way for this conversation: your previous workspace is
  left behind, and there is no tool to switch back. Pending changes go with
  it unless you name them in `keep`: those files are carried into the new
  checkout as uncommitted edits, exactly as they are now — for scratch work
  you never meant to commit, or a task you began on the wrong branch. So
  when the errand is a side quest, hand it to a staff worker (its workspace
  is a disposable copy) instead of switching yourself.

  A checked-out workspace cannot export back to the host: the only way work
  leaves it is commit + push (then `gh pr create`). Git authentication is
  handled by the API using the host's own git credentials, not the GitHub
  token.
  """
  checkout(
    llm: LLM!,
    source: Workspace!,
    """
    Git URL of the repository to check out, e.g.
    "https://github.com/dagger/otel-go".
    """
    repo: String!,
    """
    Branch to check out. Empty checks out the repository's default branch.
    """
    branch: String! = "",
    """
    Glob patterns of pending changes to carry into the new checkout, relative
    to the workspace root (e.g. "notes/**" or "scratch.md") — the same
    patterns `status` and `commit` take. Matching files land in the target
    as uncommitted edits with the content they have here, replacing whatever
    the target has at those paths; a pending deletion removes the path
    there too. Everything else pending is left behind. Fails when nothing
    pending matches, rather than switching and losing it.
    """
    keep: [String!]! = [],
    """
    Also compose the target workspace's own agents onto your toolset. Their
    modules' code and prompts come from the target repository — disable this
    when checking out a repository you do not trust.
    """
    composeAgents: Boolean! = true,
  ): LLM! @cache(policy: FunctionCachePolicy.Never) {
    # @cache(Never): resolves a live remote and rebinds the conversation; a
    # later identical call must fetch the repo as it is then.
    let ref = if (branch == "") {
      git(repo).head
    } else {
      git(repo).branch(branch)
    }
    # Force the resolution HERE, inside the tool, so a typo'd URL or branch
    # fails as this call instead of poisoning the continuation.
    let sha = ref.commitSHA rescue {
      e: Error => raise `cannot check out ${repo}: ${plainError(e.message)}`
    }
    let label = ref.name.trimPrefix("refs/heads/") rescue {
      e: Error => "HEAD"
    }

    # Pending changes selected by `keep`, resolved against the OLD workspace
    # before anything switches. An explicit selection that matches nothing
    # is refused: silently dropping the very files the agent asked to keep is
    # the loss this argument exists to prevent.
    let kept = source.git.uncommitted.filter(include: keep)
    let keptPaths = if (keep.isEmpty) {
      []
    } else {
      kept.diffStats.{{path}}.map { s => s.path }.filter { p => !p.hasSuffix("/") }
    }
    if (!keep.isEmpty and keptPaths.isEmpty) {
      raise `nothing pending matches keep: ${keep.join(", ")} (status shows what is pending)`
    }

    # A changeset applies as an overlay — files replaced wholesale, deletions
    # removed — so pending edits from one repository land in an unrelated
    # tree without needing a common baseline: the file arrives as it is here.
    let target = if (keptPaths.isEmpty) {
      ref.asWorkspace
    } else {
      let carried = ref.asWorkspace.withChanges(kept)
      # Force the overlay HERE too, for the same reason as the resolution:
      # reading the target's pending changes back evaluates the apply.
      carried.git.uncommitted.diffStats.{{path}}.length rescue {
        e: Error => raise `cannot carry pending changes into ${repo}: ${plainError(e.message)}`
      }
      carried
    }
    let keptNote = if (keptPaths.isEmpty) {
      ""
    } else {
      ` with ${toString(keptPaths.length)} pending file(s) kept: ${keptPaths.join(", ")}`
    }
    let where = `${repo}@${label} (${short(sha)})` + keptNote

    let switched = llm.withWorkspace(target)
    if (!composeAgents) {
      print(`Checked out ${where}. Your tools now operate on this checkout.`)
      return switched
    }

    # Redundancy filter: a module name the originating workspace already
    # composes is assumed to provide the same tools — only the target's OWN
    # agents are layered on. (Colliding tool names among what remains get
    # namespaced by the engine, never silently shadowed.)
    let known = source.agents.list.{{name}}.map { a =>
      a.name.split(":", 2).takeFirst.join("")
    }.uniq
    # Reading the target's agents runs the target repo's module code, which
    # can fail for reasons that shouldn't strand the checkout (a broken
    # config, settings that need a host client): fall back to the plain
    # switch.
    let group = target.agents(exclude: known)
    let extras = group.list.{{name}}.map { a => a.name } rescue {
      e: Error => return unlayered(switched, where, plainError(e.message))
    }
    if (extras.isEmpty) {
      print(`Checked out ${where}. Your tools now operate on this checkout; ` + "it contributes no additional agents.")
      return switched
    }
    let layered = group.compose(base: switched)
    # compose is lazy: force the composition HERE, so a failing target module
    # lands in this rescue instead of poisoning the continuation.
    layered.model rescue {
      e: Error => return unlayered(switched, where, plainError(e.message))
    }
    print(`Checked out ${where}. Your tools now operate on this checkout, ` + `and its own agents were composed on top: ${extras.join(", ")}.`)
    layered
  }

  # ---- helpers ------------------------------------------------------------

  # Shared toolbox for GitHub operations: git, gh, and jq, run from the root
  # of the workspace's checkout. The workspace's files are mounted so
  # `--body-file <path>` can name a file the agent wrote — including files
  # git ignores, where drafts belong — and its git directory is mounted on
  # top at .git so git — and therefore gh — sees the checkout: its origin
  # remote resolves bare numbers and {owner}/{repo} placeholders when no
  # remote is configured. Both are snapshots inside a throwaway container:
  # nothing that runs here can write back into the workspace.
  let toolbox(source: Workspace!): Container! {
    container
      .from("alpine:3.22")
      .withExec(["apk", "add", "--no-cache", "git", "jq"])
      # gh comes from its own releases, not apk: distro packages lag, and
      # GitHub retires GraphQL fields (Projects classic) faster than they
      # catch up, which breaks `gh pr view` outright.
      .withExec(["sh", "-c", installGhSh])
      # The mounted git dir arrives with whatever ownership the engine gives
      # it; keep git from refusing to read a repo it thinks is someone
      # else's.
      .withExec(["git", "config", "--global", "--add", "safe.directory", "*"])
      .withWorkdir("/workspace")
      .withMountedDirectory("/workspace", source.directory("/"))
      .withMountedDirectory("/workspace/.git", source.git.directory)
  }

  let ghVersion: String! = "2.101.0"

  let installGhSh: String! = ```
    set -eu
    case "$(uname -m)" in
      x86_64) arch=amd64 ;;
      aarch64) arch=arm64 ;;
      *) echo "unsupported architecture: $(uname -m)" >&2; exit 1 ;;
    esac
    wget -qO /tmp/gh.tgz "https://github.com/cli/cli/releases/download/v${ghVersion}/gh_${ghVersion}_linux_$arch.tar.gz"
    tar -xzf /tmp/gh.tgz -C /tmp
    install -m 0755 "/tmp/gh_${ghVersion}_linux_$arch/bin/gh" /usr/local/bin/gh
    rm -rf /tmp/gh.tgz "/tmp/gh_${ghVersion}_linux_$arch"
  ```

  # The configured remote normalized to "owner/name", or "" when none is
  # configured — the shell side (resolveRepoSh) then falls back to the
  # checkout's own origin remote, which toolbox mounts at .git.
  let remoteSlug: String! {
    if (remote == "") {
      ""
    } else {
      normalizeSlug(remote)
    }
  }

  # Shell preamble for every gh-running script: sanitize the token, then
  # resolve OWNER_REPO from the checkout's origin remote when the caller
  # left it empty (no configured remote) — normalizeSlug's normalization,
  # in sed.
  let resolveRepoSh: String! = """
    set -eu
    # Secret commands (e.g. `gh auth token`) commonly emit a trailing
    # newline; HTTP headers reject it.
    GH_TOKEN="$(printf '%s' "$GH_TOKEN" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
    export GH_TOKEN
    if [ -z "${OWNER_REPO:-}" ]; then
      origin="$(git remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null || true)"
      if [ -z "$origin" ]; then
        echo "cannot resolve the GitHub repository: no remote is configured and the checkout has no origin remote" >&2
        exit 1
      fi
      OWNER_REPO="$(printf '%s' "$origin" | sed -E -e 's#^.*github\.com[:/]##' -e 's#\.git$##' -e 's#/$##')"
    fi
  """

  # Normalize a repository reference to "owner/name" (full https/ssh
  # github.com URLs work too; anything else passes through).
  let normalizeSlug(ref: String!): String! {
    let m = ref.match(`github\.com[:/]([^/]+)/([^/]+?)(?:\.git)?/?$`)
    if (m == null) {
      ref
    } else {
      (m.captures[0] ?? "") + "/" + (m.captures[1] ?? "")
    }
  }

  # An issue or pull request reference — a bare number (resolved against
  # the configured remote; "" leaves it to resolveRepoSh and the checkout's
  # origin) or a github.com issue/PR URL — as repo + number.
  let issueRef(target: String!): {{repo: String!, number: String!}}! {
    let ref = target.trimSpace.trimPrefix("#")
    if (ref.containsMatch(`^\d+$`)) {
      return {{repo: remoteSlug, number: ref}}
    }
    let m = ref.match(`github\.com/([^/]+)/([^/]+)/(?:pull|issues)/(\d+)`)
    if (m == null) {
      raise `not an issue or pull request reference: "${target}" — pass a number or a github.com issue/PR URL`
    }
    {{repo: (m.captures[0] ?? "") + "/" + (m.captures[1] ?? ""), number: m.captures[2] ?? ""}}
  }

  # What updateComment edits, by the shape of the reference: a comment link
  # ("#issuecomment-<id>" or "#discussion_r<id>") names a comment; a plain
  # issue/PR reference names its description (kind "description", which the
  # shell side resolves to pulls/N or issues/N).
  let editRef(target: String!): {{repo: String!, number: String!, kind: String!, commentId: String!, what: String!}}! {
    let base = issueRef(target)
    let m = target.match(`#(?:issuecomment-|(?:discussion_)?r)(\d+)`)
    if (m == null) {
      return {{repo: base.repo, number: base.number, kind: "description", commentId: "", what: `the description of ${base.repo}#${base.number}`}}
    }
    let id = m.captures[0] ?? ""
    if (target.contains("#issuecomment-")) {
      {{repo: base.repo, number: base.number, kind: "issuecomment", commentId: id, what: `comment ${id} on ${base.repo}#${base.number}`}}
    } else {
      {{repo: base.repo, number: base.number, kind: "review", commentId: id, what: `review comment ${id} on ${base.repo}#${base.number}`}}
    }
  }

  # The numeric id of a review comment reference: a "#discussion_r<id>"
  # link, a bare "discussion_r<id>", or the number itself.
  let commentId(ref: String!): String! {
    let s = ref.trimSpace
    if (s.containsMatch(`^\d+$`)) {
      return s
    }
    let m = s.match(`(?:discussion_)?r(\d+)$`)
    if (m == null) {
      raise `not a review comment reference: "${ref}" — pass a #discussion_r<id> link or the comment's numeric id`
    }
    m.captures[0] ?? ""
  }

  # True when args carry an HTTP method flag (-X/--method, joined or
  # separate) naming `method`.
  let hasMethod(args: [String!]!, method: String!): Boolean! {
    let found = false
    args.each { t, i =>
      let u = t.toUpper
      if (t == "-X" or t == "--method") {
        if ((args[i + 1] ?? "").toUpper == method) {
          found = true
        }
      } else if (u.hasPrefix("--METHOD=") or u.hasPrefix("-X")) {
        if (u.trimPrefix("--METHOD=").trimPrefix("-X") == method) {
          found = true
        }
      }
    }
    found
  }

  # True when args carry gh's body flags (--body/-b, --body-file/-F).
  let hasBodyFlag(args: [String!]!): Boolean! {
    args.any { t =>
      t == "--body" or t == "-b" or t == "--body-file" or t == "-F" or t.hasPrefix("--body=") or t.hasPrefix("--body-file=")
    }
  }

  # args with `--repo slug` added — before a "--" separator when there is
  # one, so the flag stays a flag.
  let withRepoFlag(args: [String!]!, slug: String!): [String!]! {
    let before = args.takeWhile { t => t != "--" }
    before + ["--repo", slug] + args.dropFirst(before.length)
  }

  # A checkout with the target's own agents left out: the fallback when
  # reading or composing them fails, which must never strand the switch
  # itself.
  let unlayered(switched: LLM!, where: String!, reason: String!): LLM! {
    print(`Checked out ${where}. Your tools now operate on this checkout; ` + `its own agents could not be composed (${reason}).`)
    switched
  }

  # Engine errors carry a "[traceparent:<trace>-<span>]" marker so an agent
  # can go read the failing span's logs. The failures rescued here are
  # ordinary mistakes in a tool argument — a typo'd URL or branch — not
  # engine faults; drop the marker from the text that gets embedded in a
  # friendly message, keep everything the message actually says.
  let plainError(message: String!): String! {
    message.replaceMatches(`\s*\[traceparent:[^\]]*\]`, with: "").trimSpace
  }

  let short(sha: String!): String! {
    sha.split("", 8).takeFirst(7).join("")
  }

  # jq program for updateComment: given the current object (a comment or an
  # issue/PR), $old and $new (--rawfile), and $guard (non-empty when the
  # target is a comment), emit the edited body or fail with the reason.
  let replaceJq: String! = """
    def robot: test("^\\s*:robot:");
    (.body // "") as $body
    | if $guard != "" and ($body | robot | not)
      then error("refusing to edit: this comment is not agent-authored (its body does not start with \":robot:\")")
      else . end
    | ($body | indices($old) | length) as $n
    | if $n == 0
      then error("oldText not found in the current text")
      elif $n > 1
      then error("oldText matches \($n) times; include more surrounding context to make it unique")
      else ($body | split($old) | join($new)) end
    | if $guard != "" and (robot | not)
      then error("refusing to edit: the edited comment would no longer start with \":robot:\"")
      else . end
  """

  # jq program rendering a PR's review comments (a JSON array of pages, via
  # `jq -s` over `gh api --paginate`) as markdown threads. DISCUSSION_ID
  # narrows the output to the thread containing that comment.
  let renderJq: String! = """
    # A comment anchors at the LAST line of its diff hunk, so keep the tail.
    def trimhunk: split("\n")
      | if length > 24
        then ["… (" + ((length - 24) | tostring) + " earlier hunk lines truncated) …"] + .[-24:]
        else .
        end
      | join("\n");
    (env.DISCUSSION_ID // "") as $did
    | add
    | map(. + {thread: (.in_reply_to_id // .id)})
    | if $did == "" then .
      else
        ( map(select(.id == ($did | tonumber)))
          | if length == 0
            then error("comment r" + $did + " is not a review comment on this pull request")
            else .[0].thread
            end
        ) as $root
        | map(select(.thread == $root))
      end
    | if length == 0 then "No review comments."
      else
        group_by(.thread)
        | map(
            .[0] as $top
            | "### "
              + ($top.path // "(general)")
              + (if $top.line != null then ":" + ($top.line | tostring)
                 elif $top.original_line != null then ":" + ($top.original_line | tostring) + " (outdated)"
                 else ""
                 end)
              + "\n"
              + (if ($top.diff_hunk // "") != ""
                 then "\n```diff\n" + ($top.diff_hunk | trimhunk) + "\n```\n"
                 else ""
                 end)
              + (map("\n"
                     + (.user.login // "ghost")
                     + " (" + .created_at + ") discussion_r" + (.id | tostring) + ":\n\n"
                     + .body + "\n")
                 | join(""))
          )
        | join("\n---\n\n")
      end
  """

  let noTokenError: String! = """
    No GitHub token is configured, so GitHub operations are unavailable.
    Wire one up in dagger.toml:

        [env.dev.modules.contributor.settings]
        githubToken = "env://GITHUB_TOKEN"
  """

  let robotPrefixError: String! = """
    Comments posted by this tool are agent-authored and must be marked as
    such: start the body with ":robot:" so readers can tell the comment was
    written by a machine, not the human operator.
  """

  # Instructions for the agent holding these tools.
  let systemPrompt: String! = """
    You have GitHub tools: `gh` runs the GitHub CLI; `comment` and
    `updateComment` post and edit agent-marked comments; `reviewComments`
    reads a pull request's review threads; `pushBranch` pushes to a fork;
    `checkout` switches this conversation to another repository.

    Use `gh` the way a human at a terminal would, and read its output as
    they would: `gh pr view 14174`, `gh pr view 14174 --comments`, `gh pr
    diff 14174`, `gh pr checks 14174`, `gh issue list --label bug`, `gh
    issue view 8`, `gh run view <id> --log-failed`, `gh release list`.
    Reach for `--json`/`--jq` only to extract a specific field — an id, a
    URL, a head sha — and for `gh api <path>` only when the CLI has no verb
    for it (`gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/...`; {owner}/{repo} resolve to the
    configured remote or the checkout's origin). Prefer gh to fetching
    github.com pages, which render as navigation noise. Bare numbers
    resolve against the configured remote (else the checkout's origin);
    pass `--repo owner/name` for another repository.

    To file an issue or open a pull request, write the body with your
    editor to a scratch file (e.g. tmp/body.md; the workspace is mounted,
    read-only in effect, so gh can read it) and pass `--body-file`. A
    contribution is the ordinary flow made of reviewable steps: edit,
    review with the git tools, `commit`, `push` to a feature branch, then
    `gh pr create --title "..." --body-file tmp/pr.md` with a body written
    for a reviewer (add `--head owner:branch` for a branch on a fork). Edit
    a PR's metadata with `gh pr edit`/`gh pr ready`/`gh pr close` — but not
    its body: descriptions are edited with `updateComment`.

    `comment` posts on an issue or pull request: pass the target (a number
    or URL) and a body, plus optionally a `path` (and `line`) to anchor it
    to a PR's diff, or `replyTo` (a #discussion_r<id> link) to reply in a
    review thread. Comments are agent-authored, so the body must start with
    ":robot:" — the tool rejects it otherwise, and `gh pr comment`/`gh
    issue comment`/`gh pr review` are unavailable for the same reason.
    Keep feedback kind, brief, and to-the-point.

    `updateComment` edits an existing comment (an #issuecomment-<id> or
    #discussion_r<id> link) or an issue/PR description (a number or URL)
    by exact-match replacement, like editing a file: `oldText` must occur
    exactly once and is swapped for `newText`. Change only what needs
    changing. Only agent-authored comments (starting with ":robot:") can be
    edited; descriptions always can.

    `reviewComments` reads the review comment threads on a pull request —
    each with its file, line, and diff context, which `gh pr view` does not
    show. Pass a PR number or URL; the single-comment links people paste in
    conversation (ending in #discussion_r<id>) narrow the output to just
    that comment's thread. When asked to look at or address review
    feedback, read the thread first and act on what it actually says.

    `pushBranch` publishes the workspace's committed HEAD to a branch on
    ANOTHER repository — typically a fork, for when you cannot or should
    not push branches to the repository you checked out (the committer
    `push` only reaches the checkout's own remote). The fork flow is:
    checkout upstream → edit → commit → pushBranch(repo: "you/fork",
    branch: "fix-x") → `gh pr create --head you:fix-x`. Pending changes
    are not pushed: commit first.

    `checkout` replaces this conversation's workspace with a fresh checkout
    of another git repository — for when work belongs somewhere else, e.g.
    an upstream bug in a dependency. Your tools come along and simply
    operate on the new checkout, and any agents the target workspace itself
    defines are composed on top of them. The switch is one-way: your
    previous workspace is left behind, pending changes included — unless
    you name them in `keep` (glob patterns, like `commit`'s paths), which
    carries those files into the new checkout as uncommitted edits. That is
    the way out when you have scratch edits you never meant to commit, or
    realize you started from the wrong branch. A checked-out workspace
    cannot export back to the host — its work leaves only by commit + push
    — so the full errand is checkout → edit → commit → push (or pushBranch,
    to a fork) → `gh pr create`.

    Because checkout is one-way, prefer delegating the whole errand when you
    have the means to (e.g. a staff worker or sub-agent whose workspace is a
    disposable copy): tell it to checkout the repository, make the change,
    commit, push, and open the PR, and your own workspace stays bound.
    Only checkout yourself when switching repositories IS the task at hand.
  """
}
